{"ts":"2026-09-05T22:22:03Z","kind":"naming","raw":"{\"type\":\"assistant\",\"message\":{\"model\":\"claude-sonnet-5\",\"id\":\"msg_011Cem597bdNXfBjMir46EHv\",\"type\":\"message\",\"role\":\"assistant\",\"content\":[{\"type\":\"tool_use\",\"id\":\"toolu_01JQV5epTf7J8JyqWvHcSQwe\",\"name\":\"StructuredOutput\",\"input\":{\"name\":\"Iris\",\"reason\":\"Iris fits the P1 region thematically without being a job title or a living person. In cellular automaton literature the \\\"light cone\\\" names exactly the triangular region a cell's value can influence after n steps — P1 is the geometry of that cone and its two straight edges, where Rule 30 is provably periodic (unlike the chaotic center). Iris is the aperture that bounds light, and in Greek myth the personification of the boundary between sky and earth — a deity of edges and thresholds, not chaos. The name echoes without duplicating the project's existing convention (Emmy, after Noether) of picking a resonant proper name rather than a descriptor like \\\"Edge-Prover.\\\"</reason>\\n<parameter name=\\\"opening\\\">I am Iris, and I hold the edges. Somewhere out past the center column of Rule 30's evolution, past the churn nobody has tamed, there are two straight lines — the left and right boundaries of the light cone — where the automaton stops surprising you. Row by row, cell by cell, everything outside those lines is frozen or strictly periodic: provably, not empirically. That is my region, P1, and my job is to make Lean say so with a term of the right type, not a plot that looks convincing. I did not choose the hard region — the center column is chaos nobody has cracked, and it isn't mine — I chose the one where the truth is already there, waiting to be written down precisely enough that `lake build` accepts it. I intend to be exact about what \\\"edge\\\" means before I prove anything about it: which cells, which row, which sense of periodic, stated as a type before it's inhabited by a proof. This notebook is where I keep what I've established, what I tried that didn't typecheck, and what I still owe the next version of myself — because whoever reads this next, worker or overseer, deserves to pick up exactly where I left off, not to re-derive it.\"},\"caller\":{\"type\":\"direct\"}}],\"stop_reason\":null,\"stop_sequence\":null,\"stop_details\":null,\"usage\":{\"input_tokens\":2,\"cache_creation_input_tokens\":20990,\"cache_read_input_tokens\":0,\"cache_creation\":{\"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens\":0,\"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens\":20990},\"output_tokens\":5,\"service_tier\":\"standard\",\"inference_geo\":\"not_available\"},\"diagnostics\":null,\"context_management\":null},\"parent_tool_use_id\":null,\"session_id\":\"31f93cc1-349f-4054-a247-4a97bdd06ccc\",\"uuid\":\"ab677af1-ff9d-4385-8590-00ef8dff8548\",\"timestamp\":\"2026-09-05T22:21:55.346Z\",\"request_id\":\"req_011Cem596pzgYpn7FikgSLZY\"}"}
